/* data/detail-c/chem-11-10.js - GENERATED by tools/build.py from data/detail/chem-11-10.json (our own words). Do not edit. */
(window.DETAIL_C = window.DETAIL_C || {})["chem-11-10"] = {"chem-11-10-m01":{"idea":"The body needs six kinds of nutrients, and the question asks you to pick the group that lists them correctly.","steps":["Carbohydrates, fats and proteins are needed in large amounts. They give energy and build the body.","Vitamins and minerals are needed in small amounts, but they are still essential.","Water is also a nutrient, because every body process happens in it.","Only the last group has all of these."],"others":"Antibiotics, drugs and plastics are not nutrients. The groups with proteins and antibiotics only, or vitamins, minerals and drugs only, miss most of the real nutrients.","trap":"Medicines and plastics are never nutrients.","answer":"carbohydrates, fats, proteins, vitamins, minerals and water"},"chem-11-10-m02":{"idea":"Each nutrient has a main job. This question asks which one builds and repairs tissue.","steps":["Proteins are long chains of amino acids.","The body uses them to make muscle, skin, hair and enzymes.","So they are the body-building and repairing nutrients."],"others":"Carbohydrates and fats are mainly fuel for energy. Water is a medium for reactions and does not build tissue.","trap":"Do not confuse body-building (protein) with energy giving (carbohydrate, fat).","answer":"proteins"},"chem-11-10-m03":{"idea":"Fat stores more energy per gram than carbohydrate. Compare the two numbers.","steps":["Fat releases about 37 kJ per gram.","Carbohydrate releases about 17 kJ per gram.","37 is a little more than twice 17.","Fat has more C–H bonds and less oxygen, so more energy comes out when it is oxidised."],"others":"Fat is a good energy source, so zero is wrong. The two values are not equal. Saying fat gives less than half as much reverses the numbers.","trap":"Remember: fat 37 kJ/g, carbohydrate and protein 17 kJ/g.","answer":"more than twice as much, about 37 kJ against 17 kJ"},"chem-11-10-m04":{"idea":"Food goes bad because of chemical and biological changes. Find the real causes.","steps":["Microorganisms such as bacteria and fungi grow in food and break it down.","Enzymes in the food itself also decompose it.","Oxygen oxidises fats and oils and makes them rancid.","So the correct group is oxygen, enzymes and microorganisms."],"others":"Moonlight and cold air do not spoil food; cold slows spoilage. The weight of food above and the container's metal or colour are not the main causes.","answer":"oxygen, enzymes and microorganisms"},"chem-11-10-m05":{"idea":"An antioxidant protects food from slow oxidation by air.","steps":["Oxygen reacts with fats and oils and makes food rancid.","An antioxidant such as BHA or BHT reacts with oxygen first.","This protects the food, so it stays fresh longer."],"others":"A colouring agent gives colour and a sweetener gives sweetness. An antioxidant does not kill all bacteria; that is the job of preservatives and heating.","trap":"Antioxidant means against oxidation, not against bacteria.","answer":"prevent the oxidation of the food, so that it lasts longer"},"chem-11-10-m06":{"idea":"Artificial sweeteners give a sweet taste without the energy of sugar.","steps":["Saccharin and aspartame are many times sweeter than sugar.","A tiny amount gives the same sweetness.","So they add almost no energy.","This helps people with diabetes or those who want fewer calories."],"others":"They are not an energy source, so cheaper energy is wrong. They are not the same as glucose. They are safe only within permitted limits, not in any amount.","trap":"Safe does not mean unlimited. There is a permitted limit.","answer":"are many times sweeter than sugar, and give almost no energy"},"chem-11-10-m07":{"idea":"An analgesic is a pain killer. Learn the difference from an anaesthetic and an antipyretic.","steps":["Analgesic means a drug that relieves pain.","Non-narcotic analgesics like aspirin and paracetamol do not cause sleep or unconsciousness.","So it reduces pain without unconsciousness."],"others":"Reducing fever but not pain describes an antipyretic only. Killing bacteria is the job of an antibiotic or antiseptic. Causing loss of consciousness is an anaesthetic.","trap":"Analgesic = pain, antipyretic = fever, anaesthetic = loss of sensation.","answer":"reduces pain, and does not cause unconsciousness"},"chem-11-10-m08":{"idea":"Antibiotics are medicines made to act on bacteria only.","steps":["Penicillin and similar drugs attack the cell wall or life processes of bacteria.","A virus has no cell wall and uses the host cell to live.","So antibiotics do not work on viruses such as cold or flu.","They work against bacteria but not against viruses."],"others":"They do act on bacteria, so neither is wrong. They do not work on both equally. The claim viruses but not bacteria reverses the facts.","trap":"Never take antibiotics for a cold. It only makes bacteria resistant.","answer":"bacteria, but not against viruses"},"chem-11-10-m09":{"idea":"Antiseptics and disinfectants differ in strength and in where they are used.","steps":["A 0.2% phenol solution is mild, so it is safe on living tissue.","A 1% phenol solution is stronger and harms living tissue.","So an antiseptic goes on living tissue, and a disinfectant is for objects like floors and instruments."],"others":"Both kill microbes, not only viruses or only bacteria. There is a real difference, so no difference is wrong. The choice that puts antiseptics on floors and disinfectants on skin has the uses swapped.","trap":"Same chemical, different concentration, different use.","answer":"an antiseptic can be used on living tissue, and a disinfectant is for objects"},"chem-11-10-m10":{"idea":"Choose the substance that is mild enough for the skin.","steps":["Antiseptics are mild and used on cuts and skin.","Dilute iodine solution (tincture of iodine) is a common antiseptic.","Strong phenol (1%), chlorine and bleaching powder are disinfectants for floors, water and tanks."],"others":"Phenol at 1 percent is a disinfectant for floors. Chlorine gas is used to disinfect pools. Bleaching powder is used for water tanks. All three are too strong for skin.","answer":"a dilute solution of iodine, as in tincture of iodine"},"chem-11-10-m11":{"idea":"Soap has a definite chemical nature: a salt of a fatty acid.","steps":["A fat or oil is heated with NaOH or KOH.","This gives the sodium or potassium salt of a long-chain fatty acid.","Example: sodium stearate, C₁₇H₃₅COONa."],"others":"Short-chain calcium salts are not soaps. Esters of fatty acids with glycerol are the fats themselves. Sodium salts of sulfuric acid derivatives are detergents, not soaps.","trap":"Soap is a carboxylate salt; detergent is often a sulfonate or sulfate.","answer":"sodium or potassium salts of long-chain fatty acids"},"chem-11-10-m12":{"idea":"Saponification is the reaction of a fat with a strong base.","steps":["Fat + 3NaOH → 3 soap + glycerol.","The soap is the sodium salt of the fatty acid.","Glycerol is the other product."],"others":"Free fatty acids and sodium glycerate are not formed. Sodium sulfonates are detergents. Glycerol with fatty acids only misses the soap, because the base makes salts, not free acids.","answer":"sodium salts of fatty acids (soap) and glycerol"},"chem-11-10-m13":{"idea":"A soap molecule has two parts with opposite behaviour.","steps":["The –COO⁻ Na⁺ end is ionic, so it likes water. It is the hydrophilic head.","The long hydrocarbon chain is non-polar, so it likes oil and fats. It is the hydrophobic tail.","So the head loves water and the tail loves oil."],"others":"The head is ionic, not the tail, so the swapped description is wrong. Having an oil-loving head and tail, or a water-loving head and tail, would not let soap bridge oil and water.","answer":"a water-loving (hydrophilic) head and an oil-loving (hydrophobic) tail"},"chem-11-10-m14":{"idea":"Soap cleans by trapping oil inside tiny spheres called micelles.","steps":["The hydrophobic tails dissolve in the oil droplet.","The hydrophilic heads stay in the water, facing outward.","This forms a micelle with a negative charge outside.","Micelles repel each other, stay suspended, and water rinses them away."],"others":"Soap does not burn oil. It does not heat the water. It also does not chemically react with the oil to make new compounds; it only surrounds it.","answer":"surround the oil with their tails inside, and form micelles that are washed away"},"chem-11-10-m15":{"idea":"Hard water has Ca²⁺ and Mg²⁺ ions that react with soap.","steps":["2C₁₇H₃₅COONa + Ca²⁺ → (C₁₇H₃₅COO)₂Ca + 2Na⁺.","Calcium and magnesium soaps are insoluble.","They form a sticky scum, so soap is wasted and no lather forms."],"others":"Chloride ions do not form acids with soap. Soap does not decompose at once to fatty acids and glycerol. Soap is soluble in water; the problem is the insoluble scum.","answer":"they form an insoluble scum with the calcium and magnesium ions"},"chem-11-10-m16":{"idea":"Detergents work in hard water because of how their Ca²⁺ and Mg²⁺ salts behave.","steps":["Detergent heads are sulfonate or sulfate groups.","Their calcium and magnesium salts are soluble in water.","So no scum forms, and the detergent keeps cleaning in hard water."],"others":"If the salts were insoluble or scum-forming, detergents would fail like soaps. Solubility in oils is not the point. They do not decompose into soap.","answer":"are soluble in water, so no scum is formed"},"chem-11-10-m17":{"idea":"Pollution depends on whether bacteria can break the detergent down.","steps":["Straight-chain detergents are biodegradable. Bacteria digest them.","Branched-chain detergents resist bacteria.","They build up in rivers and lakes and form a stable foam.","The foam blocks oxygen and harms aquatic life."],"others":"Quick breakdown by bacteria would not cause pollution. They do not simply dissolve oxygen and make water acidic. They are not especially toxic to bacteria; the issue is that bacteria cannot break them.","answer":"are not broken down by the bacteria, and form a stable foam"},"chem-11-10-m18":{"idea":"Saponification value measures how much alkali a fat needs.","steps":["It is the mass of KOH in mg needed to saponify 1 g of fat.","So it is linked to the amount of alkali, not acid, water or oxygen.","Short-chain fats have more ester groups per gram, so a higher value."],"others":"Water lost on heating is a moisture test. Acid needed is an acid value idea. Oxygen taken in relates to the iodine or peroxide value, not saponification.","answer":"alkali (KOH) that is needed to turn a fixed mass of the fat into soap"},"chem-11-10-m19":{"idea":"Pick the false statement. Check each one.","steps":["Antibiotics kill bacteria, not viruses. A viral cold is not cured by an antibiotic.","So the statement that an antibiotic treats a viral cold is false.","Disinfectant too strong for skin, micelle formation and aspirin reducing pain and fever are all true."],"others":"A disinfectant is indeed too strong for skin. Soap does form micelles in water. Aspirin does reduce pain and fever. These are true, so they are not the answer.","trap":"Read for NOT. The false one is the answer.","answer":"an antibiotic is a good medicine for the treatment of a viral cold"},"chem-11-10-m20":{"idea":"Preservatives and heavy salt or sugar protect food by stopping microbes.","steps":["Sodium benzoate interferes with the life processes of microbes.","A lot of salt or sugar draws water out of microbe cells by osmosis.","Without water the microbes cannot grow, so food lasts longer."],"others":"Adding water would help microbes, not stop them. Making food a good home for bacteria is the opposite. Preservatives do not add nutrients.","answer":"stopping the growth of microorganisms in the food"},"chem-11-10-m21":{"idea":"Name the class of drugs that act against microbes.","steps":["Antibiotics, antiseptics and disinfectants all kill or stop the growth of microbes.","The group name is antimicrobials."],"others":"Antioxidants slow oxidation and do not act on microbes. Analgesics relieve pain. Antacids lower stomach acidity.","answer":"antimicrobials, including antibiotics, antiseptics and disinfectants"},"chem-11-10-n01":{"idea":"Use the reaction ratio. One mole of fat gives three moles of soap.","steps":["Moles of tristearin = 8.91 / 891 = 0.0100 mol.","Tristearin + 3NaOH → 3 sodium stearate + glycerol, so soap = 3 × 0.0100 = 0.0300 mol.","Mass of soap = 0.0300 × 306 = 9.18 g.","To 1 decimal place this is 9.2 g."],"trap":"Do not forget the factor 3. One fat molecule gives three soap molecules.","answer":"9.2 g"},"chem-11-10-n02":{"idea":"Energy of food is the sum of mass times energy per gram for each nutrient.","steps":["Carbohydrate: 20 × 17 = 340 kJ.","Fat: 10 × 37 = 370 kJ.","Protein: 5 × 17 = 85 kJ.","Total = 340 + 370 + 85 = 795 kJ.","In kcal: 795 / 4.184 = 190 kcal."],"trap":"Divide by 4.184 to go from kJ to kcal. Check with 4, 9, 4 kcal per gram: 80 + 90 + 20 = 190.","answer":"190 kcal"},"chem-11-10-n03":{"idea":"Dilution keeps the amount of phenol the same. Use C₁V₁ = C₂V₂.","steps":["C₁ = 5.0%, C₂ = 0.20%, V₂ = 500 mL.","V₁ = C₂V₂ / C₁ = 0.20 × 500 / 5.0.","V₁ = 100 / 5.0 = 20.0 mL.","Take 20.0 mL of stock and add water up to 500 mL."],"trap":"The answer must be less than the final volume, as the stock is stronger.","answer":"20.0 mL"},"chem-11-10-n04":{"idea":"Two soap ions are needed for each Ca²⁺ ion. Count moles first.","steps":["Moles of Ca²⁺ = 0.20 / 40 = 0.0050 mol.","2 mol soap react with 1 mol Ca²⁺, so soap = 2 × 0.0050 = 0.0100 mol.","Mass of soap = 0.0100 × 306 = 3.06 g."],"trap":"Do not use 1:1. The ratio is 2 soap to 1 calcium.","answer":"3.06 g"},"chem-11-10-n05":{"idea":"Sweetness is shared in proportion. A sweeter substance is needed in a smaller amount.","steps":["Aspartame is 160 times as sweet as sugar.","Mass needed = 5.0 g / 160 = 0.03125 g.","Convert to mg: 0.03125 × 1000 = 31.25 mg."],"trap":"Check: 31.25 mg × 160 = 5000 mg = 5.0 g. Remember to change g to mg.","answer":"31.25 mg"}};
